course: physics
lesson: 15
title: Wind and weather
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Wind is simply air on the move, and air moves from places where it presses harder to places where it presses less, like a crowd spilling out of a full room into an empty one.
explain: On a sunny afternoon at the beach the sand warms faster than the sea, the warm air above it rises, and cooler air from the sea rushes in to take its place: that is the sea breeze on your face.
explain: Moving air pushes on whatever is in its way, so it can lift a kite, flip an umbrella, slam a door or, when it is very fast, push a tree over.
explain: The wings of a plane turn the air downwards, and the air pushes back up on the wings strongly enough to hold up a giant jet.
explain: Weather forecasters watch the air pressure: high pressure usually means calm, settled days, low pressure means clouds, wind and rain.
fact: Wind is air moving from where it presses harder to where it presses less.
fact: On a sunny day the land warms faster than the sea, so warm air rises over the land and cooler sea air blows in; at night the land also cools faster than the sea, so the breeze often turns round and blows out to sea.
fact: High pressure usually brings calm, settled weather, and low pressure usually brings clouds, wind and rain.
fact: A barometer measures air pressure, and a falling barometer warns that bad weather may be coming.
fact: The push of the wind grows very quickly with its speed: wind twice as fast pushes about four times as hard.
fact: A kite flies because the wind is turned downwards by the tilted kite and pushes it up, while the string holds it at an angle.
fact: A windsock shows which way the wind blows, and pilots take off into the wind because it gives the wings more air flowing over them.
fact: An umbrella turns inside out when a gust pushes up underneath it harder than from above.
fact: A plane's wings turn air downwards and make the air over the top flow faster with lower pressure, so the air pushes the wings up; the faster the plane goes, the more lift (upward push) its wings make, so it races along the runway first.
fact: A tornado is a column of air spinning very fast with very low pressure in its middle, and its winds can rip off roofs and lift cars.
fact: In a tornado people shelter in a cellar or a small room in the middle of the house, away from windows.
fact: Wind through an open window can push a door shut with a slam, which is why a door stop keeps fingers safe.
fact: Blowing out a candle pushes the flame's hot gas away from the wick, while very gentle blowing can bring more air and make it brighter.
exercise: What is wind? | Air moving from where it presses harder to where it presses less | Air being made by the trees waving | The Earth breathing out through the sea | Clouds pushing the air as they move
exercise: Why does a breeze blow in from the sea on a sunny afternoon? | The land warms faster, warm air rises over it, and sea air rushes in | The sea warms faster and pushes air onto the land | The waves make the wind as they crash | The Sun blows the air from the sea
exercise: What weather does high pressure usually bring? | Calm, settled weather | Storms and heavy rain | Snow every time | Thunder and lightning
exercise: What does low pressure usually bring? | Clouds, wind and rain | Clear skies and no wind | Hot, still days | Fog that never lifts
exercise: What does a barometer measure? | Air pressure | Wind speed | How much rain fell | The temperature of the sea
exercise: Why can a storm blow over a big tree? | Very fast air pushes hard on its wide branches and leaves | The rain makes the tree too heavy to stand | Thunder shakes the roots loose | Lightning always knocks trees over first
exercise: If the wind blows twice as fast, how hard does it push? | About four times as hard | Exactly the same as before | Only half as hard as before | Exactly twice as hard, no more
exercise: What holds a kite up in the sky? | The wind turned downwards by the tilted kite pushes it up | The kite is lighter than air, like a balloon full of helium gas | The string pulls it upwards | The kite flaps like a bird's wing
exercise: What does a windsock at an airport tell the pilots? | Which way the wind is blowing | How many planes are coming in | How hot the runway is getting | When it is going to start raining
exercise: Why does your umbrella turn inside out in a gust? | The wind pushes up underneath it harder than from above | The rain is far too heavy for the thin cloth of the umbrella | The umbrella gets too cold and bends | The handle twists round in your hand
exercise: How do a plane's wings hold up a giant jet? | They turn air downwards, and the air pushes the wings up | The engines blow the plane straight up off the ground like a rocket | The plane is filled with light gas | The plane floats like a boat on clouds
exercise: Why must a plane race fast along the runway before it lifts off? | The faster it goes, the more lift its wings make | The wheels push it up into the air at the end of the runway | It must burn off heavy fuel first | Slow planes are not allowed off the ground
exercise: What makes a tornado so destructive? | Very fast spinning wind with very low pressure in its middle | A huge raindrop falling all at once | Hot sand blown from deserts | Lightning that runs along the ground
exercise: Where is the safest place to shelter from a tornado? | A cellar or a small room in the middle of the house | Beside a big window, so that you can watch it coming | In a car out on the open road | Under a tall tree in the garden
exercise: Why does a door slam on its own when a window is open? | Wind through the window pushes air against the door | The door is pulled shut by the cold glass of the window | The house sinks a little in the wind | The hinges spring back by themselves
exercise: Why does a candle go out when you blow on it? | Your breath pushes the flame's hot gas away from the wick | Your breath is too wet for fire | Your breath has no air in it at all | The candle wax freezes in your breath
test: My nan says when the barometer drops, take a brolly. Is she onto something? | Yes, falling pressure often warns of rain and wind | No, barometers only show the temperature, not the weather | No, a dropping barometer means sunshine | Yes, but only in summer
test: At night on the beach the breeze turns round and blows out to sea. Why? | The land cools faster than the sea at night | The tide pulls the wind out to sea along with it | The Moon pulls the air out across the water at night | The sea gets hotter in the dark than the sand does
test: Kid in a cartoon floats off holding an umbrella in a gust. What's more likely in real life? | The umbrella flips inside out and the kid stays put | The kid flies over the rooftops | The umbrella becomes a parachute upwards | The wind stops when it touches the umbrella
test: Why do pilots prefer to take off into the wind rather than with it? | More air flows over the wings, giving more lift | The wind cools the engines down so they work better | The windsock at the end of the runway tells them to | It saves the passengers from getting dizzy as they climb
test: What would happen to the sea breeze on a cloudy day when the land barely warms? | It would be weak or missing | It would blow much harder | It would blow from the land instead all day | It would turn into a tornado
test: oi, why's my kite just lying there, is it broke? | Probably not, there is too little wind to push it up | Yes, kites only work brand new | It needs to be filled with gas | The string is too short to pull it up into the wind properly
test: Someone says a tornado sucks up roofs like a giant hoover. What is closer to the truth? | Fast spinning wind and very low pressure in the middle rip and lift them | A tornado is a real pipe of air from the sky | The roofs float up because they get hot | Tornadoes are only strong enough to lift leaves
test: Which is true about how hard wind pushes? | A small rise in wind speed can make its push much bigger | Wind always pushes exactly the same, whether it is fast or slow | Slow wind pushes harder than fast wind | Wind only pushes on things that are moving
test: You blow very gently on a candle and it burns brighter. How is that possible? | A gentle breath brings fresh air to the flame | Your breath contains extra wax | Gentle blowing makes the wick grow longer and burn more | It is impossible, so you must be mistaken
test: Why is a door stop a good idea on a windy day with the windows open? | A gust can slam the door on someone's fingers | It keeps the room warmer when the windows are open | It stops the windows from breaking in the strong wind | It keeps the wind from blowing into the house at all
test: Why is it dangerous to walk through a park full of tall trees in a storm? | Strong wind can bring down branches or whole trees | Trees pull lightning down onto people, but only in summer | Trees make the wind colder | Trees block the rain and make it worse
exam: Identify the principal cause of the onshore breeze observed at the coast on a sunny afternoon. | The more rapid warming of the land relative to the sea | The motion of the tides towards the shore during the afternoon | The cooling of the land relative to the sea | The breaking of waves upon the beach
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an area of low atmospheric pressure? | It is commonly associated with cloud, wind and precipitation | It is invariably associated with clear, calm and settled conditions | It is associated exclusively with snowfall | It exerts no influence upon the weather
exam: It is given that the speed of a wind is doubled. Ascertain the approximate consequent alteration in the force it exerts. | The force is increased approximately fourfold | The force remains wholly unaltered by the change | The force is diminished to one half of its former value | The force is increased approximately twofold, and no more
exam: State the principal reason for which an aircraft commences its take-off facing into the wind. | The airflow over the wings, and therefore the lift, is thereby increased | The engines are thereby cooled | The fuel is thereby conserved entirely | The passengers are thereby protected from noise
